<p>You know, normally, I chafe at authors not providing the behind-the-scenes info of their mysteries, as that makes the idea more difficult to crit. But in this case, I think the concept can just almost stand without it, considering its inherent mystery.</p>
<p>So, without any concept of backstory or headcanon, how do you plan to end this article? You're setting up quite a story here, and the escalation of the test log/interview list will only exacerbate that. Do you plan to actually allow communication at the end? If not, how will you wrap this up in a way that doesn't leave the reader feeling short-changed?</p>

