														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4665802">
									

<p>I'm somewhat torn on this; it seems like there's a great potential for you to do something really fun here, but it's also not going to be easy to pull off well.</p>
<p>I, think my biggest concern here is that the ending feels&#8230;arbitrary? It doesn't quite feel like this is naturally escalating as a story so much as it's a series of events that eventually end?</p>
<p>My suggestion here is to really setup some stakes here: if the person knows something that the Foundation wants to know, and the Foundation knows he knows it, then you have something driving the plot forward. And, once you have that, the Foundation failing to find out and trying more things and getting desperate can be used to like, really play with what the anomaly does and how it can manage to communicate eventually.</p>
<p>Then, when the Foundation figures out what the SCP is trying to say, it actually feels impactful and grants closure.</p>
<p>There are, probably, other ways you can setup stakes here: I suggest just kinda, playing with your idea a little until you have an interesting story you can tell about this.</p>

							</div>

								
					
