			<div class="content" id="post-content-4669961">
									

<p>I should probably mention that in the month since I started this thread I've thought about and edited my article a lot, and that the ending has changed too (to something much less grand and more personal - an incident report). My goal is to write the entire thing in such a way that you could plausibly deny that the guy is an SCP at all, while still leaving enough undercurrent to make the reader interested. Whether or not I've actually managed this is&#8230; up to debate.<br />
As for the &quot;story&quot; of the article, I'm unashamed to say that there isn't really any large narrative, at least nothing that I would describe as such. I guess I don't really want to give the SCP either an origin or any kind of closure - at no point are the foundation really going to know exactly what this guy's deal is. Most of the content will consist of seeing the SCP react to various questions and events. I feel like the basic mystery set up through the article pulls its weight fairly well - although it is very hard for an author to make a fair and unbiased assessment of their own work!<br />
At any rate (although it's rather presumptuous of me) I feel like the article is edited and polished enough to get start getting criticized in its entirety.</p>
<p>EDIT: Going through the article again, I suppose there is a storyline of sorts regarding the SCP's behaviour with the foundation. The SCP deliberately set out to be captured by the foundation, but throughout the article he gets slowly more discontented as he fails to make any progress with his goal (whatever it is). That thread concludes with him flat out saying that it was a mistake and asking to be let out - a request that is rather rudely denied.</p>

