			<div class="content" id="post-content-4673190">
									

<p>Since my article has changed so much since I began this thread, I'm just going to do this bit again.</p>
<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Mystery and Empathy (for the SCP)</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A man that fails to communicate every time he tries to tell the foundation anything important. He originally contacted the foundation by calling an O5's phone, and it is not known how he came by the number. He appears to know something very important which he wants to share with the foundation, but he is of course unable to do so. I would say that the article consists of two main threads: the mystery surrounding who the SCP is and what he wants, and the discontent of the SCP as the group that he willingly went to fail to help him and refuse to let him go.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong><br />
-Special Containment Procedures<br />
-Description, including an interview log showing how the SCP fails to communicate<br />
-Addendum-A, a list of the foundation's attempts to communicate in different ways. These grow steadily more unpleasant for the SCP.<br />
-Addendum-B, a full description of the way in which the SCP was acquired (which is only hinted at before now).<br />
-Addendum-C, the quotes by the SCP that the foundation think are important. The first three focus more on the SCP's delivery and reaction to the quotes than the content itself, while the fourth is the SCP straight up stating that him going to the foundation was a mistake and begging them to set him free. (He is flatly denied).<br />
-Incident report, in which Dr [REDACTED]'s husband goes missing after she speaks to the SCP one time. it's unclear whether his disappearance is anomalous or not. Dr [REDACTED] is obviously not too happy with this, and tries to interrogate the SCP by herself. As the last line of the article puts it:<br />
&quot;Following a psychiatric evaluation on Dr [REDACTED], the use of class C amnestics was authorized. Dr [REDACTED] has since continued to capably work for the foundation.&quot;</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The growing discontent and torment of the SCP; I aim to make the reader empathize with it.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The underlying mysteries. What is the SCP, and how does it know what it does?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
